{
  "passage": "In the eighteenth century, smallpox was one of the most feared diseases in the world. It killed millions of people and left many survivors with deep scars. An English doctor named Edward Jenner noticed something interesting in the countryside. Milkmaids who had caught cowpox, a mild disease from cows, did not seem to get smallpox. In 1796 Jenner tested this idea. He took material from a cowpox sore on the hand of a milkmaid and put it into a small cut on the arm of a boy named James Phipps. The boy became slightly ill and then recovered. Some weeks later, Jenner exposed him to smallpox, and the boy did not fall sick. Jenner called this method vaccination, from vacca, the Latin word for cow. Today we know how it works. A vaccine contains dead or weakened germs, or parts of them. When it enters the body, the body produces antibodies to fight these germs, and it remembers how to make them. If the real germs attack later, the body can destroy them quickly, before they cause disease. Vaccines have since been made against many diseases, including polio, measles and tetanus. After a worldwide vaccination campaign, smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980. India was declared free of polio in 2014, after years of pulse polio drives in which every child under five was given drops.",
